INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1850,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6188398','Acute care surgery: redefining the general surgeon.','Nelson BV, Talboy GE Jr.','2010','lucky luncheon trunkey appealed saddened grabbed exhausting predecessors horrible dissuaded barrage disparaging renaming acsu acsu acsu diverted unhindered acsu happier temporizing undesignated rotates enjoys nevada studentdoctor acsu msma talboy msma brookvnelson trunkey feliciano frangou villarreal acutecaresurgery deutschman criti gosnell jacs','Abstract These are uncertain times for general surgery residents. Problems include professional burnout-diminished quality of life, declining reimbursement for services, and erosion of the breath of surgical procedures performed. As a consequence there is a shortage of general surgeons, especially in rural areas. Trauma surgeons are becoming even more scarce. The American Association for the Surgery of Trauma (AAST) is attempting to address these problems by creation of Acute Care Surgeons. This paper discusses this new type of surgical practice. Introduction When I first became interested in surgery, I was lucky enough to attend the Clinical Congress. During a medical student luncheon, I heard Donald Trunkey, MD, speak. The diversity of his surgical career appealed to me. After the lecture, I asked him, “Where can I go to train to be a surgeon like you?” His answer saddened me. “There are no places like that anymore; surgery has changed.” That summarizes the many problems facing the future of general surgery. Talking with one of my mentors in my first year of residency, I was explaining my ideal surgical practice. He grabbed a book and handed it to me, “I think you should take a look at this, it might be just what you are looking for.” It was Acute Care Surgery: Principles and Practice , edited by L.D. Britt, MD. 1 So began my research into the evolving field of Acute Care Surgery (ACS). Why is Trauma Care Losing Favor? Various elements within medical education and the health care community have contributed to the development of ACS. More women are going into surgery than ever before. More than 30% of general surgery residents in 2007 were female. 2 Trauma surgeons have found their current work environments exhausting, with decreasing reimbursement, and demands for 24-hour trauma coverage from hospital administrators and the public. There are fewer general surgeons willing to include trauma within their practices and less operative trauma. Often the trauma surgeon is treated as a second-class physician within the hospital administrative structure. 3 Generation X (born 1961–1981) is much more lifestyle conscious than their predecessors, the baby boomers. Specialties like Dermatology, ENT, Ophthalmology and other “favorable lifestyle” specialties have becoming very popular. The implementation of the 80-hour work week has made surgical specialties more appealing to students looking for a more balanced life. 4 General surgery residents have been losing interest in trauma surgery. In some quarters it is believed that “the general surgeon is a dying breed,” and that fellowship and sub-specialization are the only ways to survive. Residents also hear that trauma is non-operative, the lifestyle is horrible, and reimbursement poor. Who wants to take care of those kinds of patients anyway? Residents interested in trauma care are often dissuaded by a constant barrage of such disparaging remarks. Nationally, only 60–80 residents pursue trauma fellowships annually. Richardson and Miller surveyed senior surgery residents in 1992. More than 90% felt comfortable taking care of trauma patients; they found trauma care challenging and fun. However, they were not going to include trauma surgery in their practices after their training was completed. Poor role models were often cited as the reason for lack of interest. More than half of the respondents thought that combining trauma and critical care would make the field more attractive. Richardson concluded that, “A career in trauma divorced from General Surgery is not going to be rewarding in the long run.” 5 Open in a new tab Acute Care Surgery is likely to become an integral part of general surgery and may increase the number of trained general surgeons and trauma surgeons. A Possible Solution to Improve Trauma Care Hospitals want surgeons to provide trauma coverage and injured patients need trauma surgeons. Level I and Level II trauma care status are great advertising. Patients feel that they are going to a hospital where their trauma can be comprehensively treated. There is also mounting evidence that patients do better when taken to centers with certain resources in place. Patients who present to Level I trauma centers have a decreased mortality from trauma. Round the clock in-house critical care staff and intensivist-managed ICU care have also been shown to decrease mortality. 6 In 2003 an ad hoc committee of the American Association for the Surgery of Trauma (AAST) published a proposal for restructuring the role of the trauma surgeon, renaming the field acute care surgery. ACS would encompass elective and emergency general surgery, trauma surgery, and surgery critical care. This new paradigm would use shift-based 24-hour coverage schedules. The goal of the program would be to provide the ACS physician with operative experience in general trauma, an elective surgery practice and some subspecialty procedures associated with trauma. The ACS program would be flexible enough to tailor the practice to the needs and desires of the institution and acute care surgeon (ACSu). 5 The ACSu would have a greatly enhanced opportunity for a quality and relatively controlled lifestyle. Since 2003, there has been a rapid increase in the demand for the ACSu throughout the United States in both academic and private practice. This includes surgeons trained in surgical critical care and trauma and surgical hospitalists, who are general surgeons that provide emergency general surgery care. The ACS model provides hospitals with an in-house surgeon around the clock. Inconvenient and disruptive ER calls to general surgeons are diverted thus allowing elective cases to proceed unhindered during the day. There is increased reimbursement to the hospital and the ACSu, and better patient care. The acute care surgeons and the elective general surgeons they work with are happier and practice more efficiently***. Some major academic centers, including Vanderbilt, University of Kentucky, University of Pennsylvania, Yale, and University of Colorado, have been developing ACS/Emergency General Surgery Services (EGS) as early as the 1990s. Post-implementation studies at these institutions noted increased satisfaction of the surgery residents, fellows, and trauma surgeons. Yale noted an operative billing increase of 45%; Vanderbilt increased their surgical admissions by 30%. 3 , 7 They found emergent cases were typically performed between 4:00 p.m. and 7:00 p.m. with trauma cases performed between 10:00 p.m. and 2:00 a.m. with little overlap. 7 Enthusiastic recommendations from these institutions contributed to the design of the new ACS fellowship. It was noted that increased bedside procedural training, interventional radiologic procedures, more thoracic and vascular experience, and some exposure to limited neurosurgical procedures and orthopedic temporizing procedures would be beneficial skills for the EGS/ACS teams. Academic institutions have found that implementation of the EGS and the hiring of acute care surgeons provide much needed relief for generalists, increased operating room efficiency, and decreased patient wait time. These institutions found that 60% of their trauma staff were already working as “emergency surgeons,” and their roles within the institution did not require major restructuring. Community hospitals have also integrated ACS successfully. For example, one acute care surgeon (trained in surgery critical care) covers all emergent cases during the day and the limited critical care needs for a seven-surgeon group. This surgeon also covers any undesignated consults. The surgeon group rotates night and weekend call. The Emergency Department at this hospital has been pleased to have a surgeon immediately available to see consultations and the patients have been happy for the decreased waiting time for evaluation and operations. This acute care surgeon enjoys the wide variety within the practice and the partners enjoy having much less disruption to their daily schedules and elective cases. Three ACS fellowship programs were certified by the AAST in 2008: the Universities of Colorado, Nevada, and Pittsburgh. Recently added programs include the University of California-San Francisco, University of Maryland Shock-Trauma Center, and Massachusetts General Hospital. 5 There are more than 20 programs in the process of applying for ACS certification. Many other non-AAST approved fellowships in ACS exist including Johns Hopkins and Washington University. AAST-approved programs are required to include certain elements within their curriculum and complete an evaluation and inspection process by AAST; they must also already have a well-established surgery critical care program. Concerns with the ACS/EGS structure have included possible decreased revenue and referrals to elective general surgeons. This has not been the case at institutions implementing these programs. Orthopedic and neurosurgeons have expressed concerns about expanding the scope of practice of ACS and have been reluctant to participate in teaching their specialty-specific procedures. The idea of a more lifestyle conscious surgical specialty appeals to many medical students, especially women interested in surgery. An ongoing thread has developed on www.studentdoctor.net just to discuss ACS. With greater than 20 institutions awaiting AAST certifications and many institutions developing sections within their departments, it is evident clear ACS enhances surgical care and trauma and is academically robust. Many private hospitals have developed emergency general surgery teams staffed by ACSu. Surgical journals have multiple advertisements for acute care surgery positions throughout the United States. Conclusion The ACS movement initiated in the late 1990s, and defined in 2003 by the AAST, has provided a unique opportunity to redefine and enhance the role of the general surgeon within the health care system. ACS has offered an appealing revision of the ‘surgical lifestyle’ to contemporary surgical residents and medical students. ACS is likely to become an integral part general surgery and may increase the number of trained general surgeons and trauma surgeons. Biography Brook V. Nelson, MD, MSMA member since 2007, is a General Surgery Resident, Department of Surgery, University of Missouri-Kansas City School of Medicine. Glenn E. Talboy, Jr., MD, FACS, MSMA member since 2010, is Interim Chair, Department of Surgery, Program Director, Associate Professor of Surgery, University of Missouri-Kansas City School of Medicine. Contact: brookvnelson@gmail.com Open in a new tab Footnotes Disclosure None reported. References 1. Britt LD, Trunkey D, Feliciano D, editors. Acute Care Surgery: Principles and Practice. 2007. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Frangou C. Critical Mass: With the Stakes High. Women May Assume Critical Role in Surgery’s Future General Surgery News. 2008 Oct [ Google Scholar ] 3. Cohn S, Price M, Villarreal CL. Trauma and Surgical Critical Care Workforce in the United States: A Severe Surgeon Shortage Appears Imminent. J Am Coll Surg. 2009 Oct;209(4):446–451. doi: 10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2009.06.369. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Anderson J. Experts Urge Work Hour Flexibility Surgery News. Dec, 2009. [ Google Scholar ] 5. [Accessed April 30, 2010]. http://www.aast.org/Library/AcuteCareSurgery/Default.aspx . 6. Hanson CW, Deutschman C, Anderson H, Reilly P. Effects of an organized critical care service on outcomes and resource utilization: A cohort study. Criti Care Med. 1999 Feb;27(2):270–274. doi: 10.1097/00003246-199902000-00030. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Maa J, Gosnell J, Carter J, Wachter R, Harris H. The surgical hospitalist: A new solution for emergency surgical care? JACS Bulletin. 2007 Nov;92(11):8–17. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',11,'2025-10-13 08:19:07','2025-10-13 08:19:07');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1851,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11272338','How Did Black and Hispanic Orthopaedic Applicants and Residents Compare to General Surgery Between 2015 and 2022?','Williams AJ, Malewicz JI, Pum JM, Zurakowski D, Day CS.','2024','totaled totaled datapoints scatterplot acceptances outpacing abelson abelson corr b291 totalled totaled othopaedic datapoints scatterplots trendlines conciseness scatterplot scatterplot scatterplot scatterplot overestimating abelson llado farrulla nehemiah intiative revolve abjs 64kb abjs certifies consultancies alisha alisha malewicz malewicz pum jpum1 hfhs zurakowski zurakowski childrens abelson symer adelani e96 marcotte boatright s28 yiadom blakemore biermann boylan shinkai secrist scannell patt crites shanks e25962 rarest deboever swisher vaporciyan hetz antonoff e113 alleyne bervell hemal reghunathan newsom gosman mosquera e2021050964 kalyanasundaram mener dicaprio gruppuso kalagara eltorai depasse e114 llado farrulla fosnot couto lubowitz mankin waj nth replogle e0007 meadows faraj mylavarapu gudi mylavarapu omb orwh omb nehemiah ode okike utuk e107 pinpin nellans bitterman mulcahey e23 nellans gorroochurn chahine esidency rama ekhtiari thevendran khanduja ramkumar chughtai haeberle mont rubright jodoin akelman ruddell sudah imam sirch namdari tabaie schermerhorn lattanza ode mefsin trikha keswani ishmael greig bernthal e24 quickfacts rhi625219 heest agel e64 wijesekera sorenson murthi aleem zmistowski abjs 64kb abjs','Abstract Background Despite the heavy demand for and knowledge of the benefits of diversity, there is a persistent lack of racial, ethnic, and gender diversity in orthopaedic surgery. Since the implementation of diversity initiatives, data have shown that general surgery has been one of the top competitive surgical fields and has demonstrated growth in racial, ethnic, and gender diversity, making general surgery a good point of reference and comparison when analyzing racial and ethnic growth in orthopaedic surgery. Questions/purposes (1) What were the growth rates for Black and Hispanic orthopaedic residency applicants and residents between 2015 and 2022? (2) How did the growth rates of Black and Hispanic individuals in orthopaedic surgery compare with those of general surgery? (3) How did applicant recruitment and resident acceptance differ between Black and Hispanic people in orthopaedic surgery? Methods Applicant data were obtained from historical specialty-specific data from the Association of American Medical Colleges Electronic Residency Application Service Statistics database between 2018 and 2022, and resident data were obtained from the Accreditation Council of Graduate Medical Education Data Resource Book between 2015 and 2021. Between 2018 and 2022, the number of residency applicants totaled 216,677, with 17,912 Black residency applicants and 20,413 Hispanic residency applicants. Between 2015 and 2021, the number of active residents totaled 977,877, with 48,600 Black residents and 62,605 Hispanic residents. Because the applicant and resident data do not overlap throughout all years of observation, a sensitivity analysis of overlapping years (between 2018 and 2021) was conducted to ensure observed trends were consistent and valid throughout the study. All datasets obtained were used to establish the different racial and ethnic proportions of Black and Hispanic residency applicants and residents in four nonsurgical primary care specialties and four surgical subspecialties. A reference slope was created using data from the Association of American Medical Colleges and Accreditation Council of Graduate Medical Education to represent the growth rate for total residency applicants and residents, independently, across all residency specialties reported in each database. This slope was used for comparison among the resident and applicant growth rates for all eight selected specialties. Datapoints were placed into a scatterplot with regression lines, using slope equations to depict rate of growth and R 2 values to depict linear fit. Applicant growth corresponded to applicant recruitment and resident growth corresponded to resident acceptance. Chi-square tests were used to compare residents and residency applicants for the Black and Hispanic populations, separately. Two-way analysis of variance with a time-by-specialty interaction term (F-test) was conducted to determine differences between growth slopes. Results There was no difference in the growth rate of Black orthopaedic surgery applicants between 2018 and 2022, and there was no difference in the growth rate of Hispanic orthopaedic surgery applicants (R 2 = 0.43; p = 0.23 and R 2 = 0.63; p = 0.11, respectively). However, there was a very slight increase in the growth rate of Black orthopaedic surgery residents between 2015 and 2021, and a very slight increase in the growth rate of Hispanic orthopaedic surgery residents (R 2 = 0.73; p = 0.02 and R 2 = 0.79; p = 0.01, respectively). There were no differences in orthopaedic and general surgery rates of growth for Black applicants between 2018 and 2022 (0.004 applicants/year versus -0.001 applicants/year; p = 0.22), and no differences were found in orthopaedic and general surgery rates of growth for Black residents between 2015 and 2021 (0.003 residents/year versus 0.002 residents/year; p = 0.59). Likewise, Hispanic orthopaedic applicant growth rates did not differ between 2018 and 2022 from the rates of general surgery (0.004 applicants/year versus 0.005 applicants/year; p = 0.68), and there were no differences in orthopaedic and general surgery rates of growth for Hispanic residents (0.007 residents/year versus 0.01 residents/year; p = 0.35). Furthermore, growth rate comparisons between Black orthopaedic applicants and residents between 2018 and 2021 showed applicant growth was larger than resident growth, illustrating that the recruitment of Black applicants increased slightly more rapidly than resident acceptance. Growth rate comparisons between Hispanic applicants and residents showed a larger rate of resident growth, illustrating Hispanic resident acceptance increased slightly faster than applicant recruitment during that time. Conclusion We found low acceptance of Black residents compared with the higher recruitment of Black applicants, as well as overall low proportions of Black and Hispanic applicants and residents. Future studies might explore the factors contributing to the higher acceptances of Hispanic orthopaedic residents than Black orthopaedic residents. Clinical Relevance We recommend that more emphasis should be placed on increasing Black and Hispanic representation at the department level to ensure cultural considerations remain at the forefront of applicant recruitment. Internal or external reviews of residency selection processes should be considered, and more immersive, longitudinal orthopaedic surgery clerkships and research mentorship experiences should be targeted toward Black and Hispanic students. Holistic reviews of applications and selection processes should be implemented to produce an increased racially and ethnically diverse applicant pool and a diverse residency work force, and implicit bias training should be implemented to address potential biases and diversity barriers that are present in residency programs and leadership. Introduction Orthopaedic surgery is one of the most competitive residency specialties each year, with the number of applications far outpacing the number of residency positions available [ 14 , 28 , 30 , 49 , 52 ]. However, despite the heavy demand for and knowledge of the benefits of racial and gender diversity, previous studies have continued to illustrate there is a lack of diversity in orthopaedic surgery [ 4 , 12 , 16 , 17 , 20 , 22 , 31 , 33 , 39 , 48 , 51 , 52 , 54 ]. A study from 2021 found that although the number of Hispanic orthopaedic surgery residents had increased substantially, it would take 93 years to achieve representation comparable to that of the United States population [ 10 ]. Additionally, Black residents constitute only 4% of orthopaedic trainees [ 47 ]. In comparison, Abelson et al. [ 1 ] assessed the numbers of Black and Hispanic residents and their presence in general surgery after the implementation of initiatives that were designed to increase diversity; they found success in increasing the population of Hispanic residents but found no difference in Black residents. From 2005 to 2014, the proportion of Black residents in general surgery increased by 21%, and the proportion of Hispanic residents increased by 46.7% [ 1 ]. Earlier published studies regarding lack of increases in racial, ethnic, and gender diversity among orthopaedic surgery residents provided analysis of trends before and after the implementation and emphasis of diversity initiatives in the early 2000s [ 1 , 4 , 10 , 12 , 17 , 20 , 22 , 31 , 33 ]. It would be interesting to evaluate the trends in applicant diversity in orthopaedics when diversity initiatives had been underway. A recent study found that applicants from racial and ethnic minority groups have lower odds of admission to orthopaedic surgery residency, even when accounting for academic performance metrics [ 41 ]. Additionally, for the 5-year 2017 to 2021 application cycles, only 18.1% of orthopaedic surgery applicants were underrepresented in medicine (URiM) among more than 7000 total applicants [ 55 ]. The populations of URiM applicants include the following categories: Black/African American, Hispanic/Latino/of Spanish origin, American Indian/Alaskan Native, and Native Hawaiian/other Pacific Islander [ 55 ]. According to the Association of American Medical Colleges (AAMC), for the 2018 to 2019 academic year, 6.2% of medical school graduates were Black/African American and 5.3% were Hispanic [ 9 ]. Although numerous studies have examined different attributes of applicants versus residents, including academic merit and gender, few have evaluated differences in race and ethnicity [ 40 , 49 ]. Recent studies have also evaluated URiM racial groups as a whole; however, contrary to the findings of Abelson et al. [ 1 ] in general surgery, no paper has separately evaluated the growth rates of Black and Hispanic applicants compared with the current makeup of orthopaedic residents [ 9 , 40 , 55 ]. Furthermore, there is controversy about what has been published. Some articles showed that orthopaedic surgery has not seen growth in terms of resident representation of minorities [ 4 , 16 , 17 , 27 ]. However, other studies showed that orthopaedic surgery has experienced increases in diversity [ 39 , 47 , 51 ]. With studies dating to 1999 emphasizing the need for diversity in orthopaedics [ 21 , 26 ], it remains unclear whether that emphasis has changed the demography of orthopaedic surgery. Therefore, examining current growth and representation trends of both Black and Hispanic applicants and residents is essential to further the discussion on this important topic in orthopaedic surgery. We therefore asked: (1) What were the growth rates for Black and Hispanic orthopaedic residency applicants and residents between 2015 and 2022? (2) How did the growth rates of Black and Hispanic individuals in orthopaedic surgery compare with those of general surgery? (3) How did applicant recruitment and resident acceptance differ between Black and Hispanic people in orthopaedic surgery? Patients and Methods Study Design and Setting This was a retrospective study drawn from large, national, longitudinally maintained databases. Applicant data were obtained from historical specialty-specific data from the AAMC Electronic Residency Application Service (ERAS) Statistics database between 2018 and 2022 [ 6 ]. The Accreditation Council of Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) Data Resource Book was used to obtain data on annual active resident characteristics between 2015 and 2021 [ 2 ]. The ERAS is used for residency application development and distribution [ 6 ], and the ACGME Data Resource Book was specifically developed as a point of reference to help identify and clarify issues pertaining to the accreditation of graduate medical education programs [ 2 ]; both databases serve as robust, reliable, and suitable data resources. Data on fellowship statistics were not included. Data Management and Variables Data that were obtained and reviewed were deidentified for individuals and institutions by the original databases, depicting specialty-specific information. Analyzed data were divided into two groups: residents and residency applicants. All data obtained were used to establish the different racial and ethnic proportions of residency applicants and active residents in four nonsurgical primary care specialties and four surgical subspecialties. To build on the foundations of published studies in the area of diversity, the medical and surgical specialties chosen for comparison were based on selections from published articles related to ethnic and racial diversity in orthopaedic surgery, including internal medicine, family medicine, obstetrics and gynecology, pediatrics, general surgery, orthopaedic surgery, otolaryngology, and neurologic surgery [ 11 , 17 , 34 , 48 , 54 ]. Race or ethnicity was self-reported by each participant and was defined as White, Black/African American, Asian, Hispanic/Latino/of Spanish origin, American Indian/Alaska Native, Native Hawaiian/other Pacific Islander, other race/ethnicity, and unknown race/ethnicity, per the AAMC FACTS glossary, ACGME Resource Data Book, United States Census Bureau, and Office of Management and Budget Standards [ 6 , 21 , 36 , 53 ]. Furthermore, per the ACGME, a category for multiple races was added and defined as individuals who self-identify as not Hispanic but identify as more than one race [ 3 ]. To obtain a snapshot of which racial or ethnicity groups constitute URiM, we analyzed the total URiM population in 2021 among residents and residency applicants. The AAMC defines URiM as any United States citizen or permanent resident self-identifying as Black/African American, Hispanic/Latino/of Spanish origin, American Indian/Alaska Native, or Native Hawaiian/other Pacific Islander [ 7 ]. For clarity, this project referred to the Black/African American group as Black and referred to the Hispanic/Latino/of Spanish origin group as Hispanic. Subsequently, we used ACGME demographic data to analyze growth in the Black and Hispanic resident populations in the eight selected medical and surgical specialties between 2015 and 2021. This process was continued using AAMC demographic data to analyze the growth of Black and Hispanic residency applicants between 2018 and 2022. We requested applicant data from the AAMC for earlier years, but received no response and we were not provided the data. Because the applicant and resident data do not overlap throughout all years of observation, a sensitivity analysis of overlapping years (between 2018 and 2021) was conducted to ensure that observed trends were consistent and valid throughout the study (Supplemental Digital Content 1; http://links.lww.com/CORR/B291 ). Descriptive Data Per ERAS, between 2018 and 2022, the number of residency applicants totalled 216,677, with an annual average of 43,335 residency applicants. The total number of Black residency applicants was 17,912, and the number of Hispanic residency applicants was 20,413 (annual averages of 3582 and 4083, respectively). Per ACGME, between 2015 and 2021, the number of active residents totaled 977,877, with an annual average of 139,697 residents. The total number of Black residents was 48,600 and the number of Hispanic residents was 62,605 (annual averages of 6943 and 8944, respectively). According to available 2021 data, among the total population of residents and residency applicants, Black residents accounted for 28% (217 of 789) of the total URiM resident population and Hispanic residents accounted for 40% (314 of 789), whereas Black applicants accounted for 33% (148 of 444) of the total URiM residency applicants and Hispanic applicants accounted for 37% (162 of 444). Comparing Black orthopaedic residents with Black orthopaedic residency applicants illustrated that there was a larger percentage of applicants than residents in 2021 (33% [148 of 444] versus 28% [217 of 789]; p = 0.03). There was no difference in the percentages of Hispanic residents and Hispanic residency applicants in orthopaedic surgery during that time (40% [314 of 789] versus 37% [162 of 444]; p = 0.25). Ethical Approval Institutional board review exemption was received because this project does not meet the definition of human subjects research and does not involve the use of a test article. Statistical Analysis Our primary study goal was to determine the growth rates of Black and Hispanic othopaedic applicants and residents. To do this, we obtained data on the annual number of applicants and residents and converted these data to annual proportions and ratios. Data for general surgery, neurologic surgery, otolaryngology, and all medical and surgical specialties (the reference slope) were added to help gauge findings and emphasize context. Reference slopes were incorporated to represent the growth rates for total active residents and total residency applicants across all medical and surgical residency specialties. The reference slope for total active residents was created using data obtained from ACGME Resource Data Books [ 2 ], and a similar reference slope for total residency applicants was generated from AAMC ERAS data. All datapoints were used to create scatterplots with regression lines for all four orthopaedic populations. Trendlines, slope equations, and R 2 values were identified for their respective populations. Analysis of variance was conducted to determine the significance of each individual slope. Our second study goal was to compare growth slopes between Black and Hispanic applicant and resident populations in orthopaedics and general surgery. To do this, we repeated the steps from the first study goal and conducted two-way analysis of variance with a time-by-specialty interaction term (F-test) to determine differences between growth slopes. Because our third study goal was to compare growth rates between Black orthopaedic applicants and residents, as well as the growth rates between Hispanic orthopaedic applicants and residents, we used two-way analysis of variance with a time-by-specialty interaction term to determine differences between growth slopes and conducted a sensitivity analysis of the overlapping observation periods to confirm the validity of our findings. Applicant growth corresponded to applicant recruitment and resident growth corresponded to resident acceptance. Chi-square tests were also conducted to compare resident and applicant cross-sectional data for the Black and Hispanic populations identified in 2021. Significance was set with an alpha of 0.05. Additional data for pediatrics, family medicine, internal medicine, and obstetrics and gynecology was initially included; however, because the reference slope incorporated data for all residency specialties, the data for those four specialties were removed to eliminate confusion and ensure clarity and conciseness. Results Growth Rates for Black and Hispanic Residency Applicants and Residents There was no difference in the growth rate of Black orthopaedic surgery applicants (0.004 applicants/year [95% CI -0.005 to 0.013]; R 2 = 0.43; p = 0.23) between 2018 and 2022 (Fig. 1 ). There was a very slight increase in the growth rate of Black orthopaedic surgery residents (0.003 residents/year [95% CI 0.000 to 0.005]; R 2 = 0.73; p = 0.02) between 2015 and 2021 (Fig. 2 ). There was also a very slight increase in the growth rate of Hispanic orthopaedic surgery residents (0.007 residents/year [95% CI 0.003 to 0.012]; R 2 = 0.79; p = 0.01) (Fig. 3 ). However, there was no difference in the growth rate of Hispanic orthopaedic surgery applicants (0.004 applicants/year [95% CI -0.002 to 0.01]; R 2 = 0.63; p = 0.11) (Fig. 4 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab This scatterplot shows regression lines comparing growth rates of Black residency applicants across surgical residencies, with no difference between orthopaedic surgery and general surgery (p = 0.22). Slope values correlated with growth rates. Slope equations and R 2 values are included in the legend for each specialty. Statistical significance was defined as p < 0.05. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab This scatterplot shows regression lines illustrating Black resident growth rates across surgical residencies, showing no difference between orthopaedic surgery and general surgery (p = 0.591). Slope values correlated with growth rates. Slope equations and R 2 values are included in the legend for each specialty. Statistical significance was defined as p < 0.05. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab This scatterplot shows regression lines comparing Hispanic resident growth rates across surgical residencies, with no difference between orthopaedic surgery and general surgery (p = 0.354). Slope values correlated with growth rates. Slope equations and R 2 values are included in the legend for each specialty. Statistical significance was defined as p < 0.05. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab This scatterplot shows regression lines comparing growth rates of Hispanic residency applicants across surgical residencies, with no difference between orthopaedic surgery and general surgery (p = 0.679). Slope values correlated with growth rates. Slope equations and R 2 values are included in the legend for each specialty. Statistical significance was defined as p < 0.05. Comparing Growth Rates of Black and Hispanic Residents Between Orthopaedic and General Surgery There were no differences in growth rates between Black applicants to orthopaedics (0.004 applicants/year) and Black orthopaedic residents (0.003 residents/year) (p = 0.22) compared with the rates of growth in general surgery of Black applicants (-0.001 applicants/year) (Fig. 1 ) and Black residents (0.002 residents/year) (p = 0.59) (Fig. 2 ). Likewise, Hispanic orthopaedic applicant (0.004 applicants/year) and Hispanic orthopaedic resident rates of growth (0.007 residents/year) did not differ over that time (p = 0.68) compared with the rates of Hispanic applicants in general surgery (0.005 applicants/year) (Fig. 4 ) and Hispanic residents in general surgery (0.01 residents/year) (p = 0.35) (Fig. 3 ). Growth Comparisons of Black Orthopaedic Applicants Versus Residents and Hispanic Orthopaedic Applicants Versus Residents Between 2018 and 2022, the growth rate of Black orthopaedic applicants was slightly larger than the growth rate of Black orthopaedic residents between 2015 and 2021 (0.004 applicants/year and 0.003 residents/year, respectively; p < 0.001), illustrating that the recruitment of Black applicants increased slightly more quickly than resident acceptance. On the other hand, between 2015 and 2021, the growth rate for Hispanic orthopaedic residents was slightly larger than the rate of growth for Hispanic applicants to orthopaedic surgery (0.007 residents/year and 0.004 applicants/year, respectively; p = 0.003), illustrating that the acceptance of Hispanic residents increased slightly more quickly than applicant recruitment during that time. The sensitivity analysis of the overlapping years of 2018 and 2021 illustrated the same trend. Discussion Orthopaedic surgery remains one of the most competitive residency specialties [ 14 , 28 , 30 , 49 , 52 ], but earlier studies have shown a lack of racial and ethnic diversity [ 4 , 12 , 16 , 17 , 20 , 22 , 31 , 33 , 39 , 48 , 51 , 52 , 54 ]. By evaluating the current trends of Black and Hispanic growth in orthopaedic surgery, we can gauge whether initiatives to increase racial and ethnic diversity have been effective. No differences were found in growth slopes for orthopaedic Black and Hispanic applicants between 2018 and 2022, but very slight increases were found for orthopaedic Black and Hispanic residents. This trend in Black and Hispanic applicants and residents was no different from applicants and residents in general surgery. Yet, our data suggest that the acceptance rate of Black orthopaedic residents remained below the recruitment rate of Black orthopaedic applicants, while the acceptance rate of Hispanic orthopaedic residents remained higher than the recruitment rate of Hispanic applicants overall. These findings can be used to emphasize the need for targeted efforts toward increasing Black resident growth and Hispanic applicant growth in orthopaedic surgery. Limitations First, data obtained from the AAMC and ACGME were self-reported and are subject to self-reporting bias and potential reporting errors, especially if individuals were multiracial or chose not to identify their race or ethnicity and instead self-report as “other” or “unknown.” This might impact the apparent growth trend among medical and surgical specialties by producing underestimated findings relative to actual trends. However, because the AAMC and ACGME represent all of their respective accredited programs as well as the associated applicants and residents, we feel that the high quantity of response rates for applicants and residents adequately represents the samples and therefore are trustworthy for this purpose. Second, the ACGME had no available resident data after 2021, limiting the timeline of resident analysis to 7 years, while the AAMC had no available residency applicant data before 2018, limiting the timeline of applicant analysis to 5 years. Although this could produce concerns regarding the validity of the study, we believe the AAMC and ACGME databases are the most valid and reliable resources for selecting data. In addition, we performed a sensitivity analysis for overlapping years (2018 to 2021) to confirm the findings, and based on this, we feel our results remain valid. Third, the Black and Hispanic orthopaedic applicant findings consisted of a shorter observation period because we were unable to obtain earlier data from the AAMC. This could have impacted the growth rate findings by overestimating or underestimating results. However, we speculate that including data from earlier years would have produced similar findings, given that the observation period had only a 3-year difference (2015 to 2018), and earlier research about orthopaedic demographics did not provide evidence of drastic increases in the Black and Hispanic populations [ 39 , 47 , 51 ]. Discussion of Key Findings We found a very slight increase in the rate of acceptance of Black and Hispanic orthopaedic surgery residents between 2015 and 2021. The growth of Black orthopaedic residents would increase at a rate of 3 residents every 1000 years and Hispanic orthopaedic residents would increase at a rate of 7 residents every 1000 years. Although there has been incremental growth in Black and Hispanic residents in orthopaedic surgery, if measures are not implemented to create more substantial increases in these populations, racial and ethnic diversity in orthopaedic surgery is unlikely to meaningfully improve for hundreds of years. This reasoning similarly applies to the Black and Hispanic orthopaedic applicant populations, because there were no differences in recruitment rates between 2018 and 2022. When we compared orthopaedic surgery and general surgery from 2018 to 2022, we found no differences in Black and Hispanic applicant growth rates. We also found no differences in the rates of growth between orthopaedic and general surgery Black and Hispanic residents between 2015 and 2021. Although Abelson et al. [ 1 ] did not examine growth rates, they found an increase in the number of Hispanic residents in general surgery between 2005 and 2015, but no differences in the number of Black residents in general surgery. Our study found similar results, because the percentage of Hispanic orthopaedic residents demonstrated a greater degree of change between 2015 and 2021 than did the percentage of Black orthopaedic residents. However, our findings illustrated that there were larger percentages of Black and Hispanic individuals in general surgery than in orthopaedic surgery. This difference between Black and Hispanic applicants and residents in orthopaedics and general surgery may have been caused by the use of holistic reviews in general surgery [ 37 ], which refers to admissions or selection processes that consider applicant experiences, attributes, and academic metrics as well as the value an applicant contributes to learning, practice, and teaching [ 8 ]. Nevertheless, our findings illustrate that orthopaedic Black and Hispanic applicant and resident growth is behind that of general surgery, requiring drastic increases in growth rates to match their respective populations in general surgery. Our study was observational and we cannot comment on the causes of the observed findings. However, prior studies have identified barriers experienced by individuals in URiM groups [ 5 , 13 , 23 , 32 , 33 , 38 , 42 , 44 - 46 , 50 , 56 , 57 ], such as lack of minority faculty or leadership representation [ 33 ], and explored different methods for improvement. Based on those studies and our findings, our recommendations are as follows: Because faculty and leadership greatly influence acceptance rates in residency programs, there should be more emphasis on increasing Black and Hispanic representation at the department level to ensure that cultural considerations remain at the forefront of application reviews and interviews [ 13 , 18 , 25 , 33 , 42 , 43 ]. McDonald et al. [ 33 ] found that a lack of minority faculty members was considered the greatest barrier to recruiting applicants from URiM groups. To address the lack of orthopaedic growth for Black and Hispanic applicants, internal and/or external reviews of residency program selection processes should be considered. Eisenberg et al. [ 19 ] found that internal program reviews identified blind spots and areas of potential bias, permitting improvement for a more diverse resident population. Immersive, longitudinal clerkships and research mentorship experiences in orthopaedic surgery should also be used and targeted toward Black and Hispanic students, allowing students access to early exposure and professional development [ 23 , 24 , 29 , 32 , 47 , 50 ]. Llado-Farrulla et al. [ 29 ] saw an increase in the number of residents from URiM groups in plastic and reconstructive surgery between 2011 and 2020 after a 4-week clerkship and holistic review. To enhance efforts toward increasing a racially and ethnically diverse applicant pool and resident population, holistic reviews should implemented. Nehemiah et al. [ 37 ] found that holistic reviews in general surgery resulted in increased numbers of matched women applicants and matched applicants from URiM groups between 2013 and 2020. Implicit bias training has been identified as a valuable resource for addressing potential biases and diversity barriers, and should be implemented for residency programs and leadership [ 15 , 25 , 35 , 43 ]. Hoff et al. [ 25 ] found that the number of matched pediatric residents from URiM groups increased after increasing the visibility of URiM faculty and implementing formal implicit bias training and holistic reviews as part of a 4-year intiative to increase racial and ethnic diversity. Conclusion Prior studies have found that racial and ethnic diversity in orthopaedic surgery is not increasing [ 1 , 4 , 17 ]. Our 7-year analysis of residency applicant and resident data illustrated that although orthopaedic Black and Hispanic applicant rates of growth did not increase, there were very slight increases in growth rates for Black and Hispanic orthopaedic residents. Although most programs may believe they are conducting holistic reviews, our data suggest there could still be characteristics that are not being considered or emphasized that are inhibiting orthopaedic surgery from making substantial increases in their Black and Hispanic resident populations. We recommend increasing targeted efforts toward recruitment and acceptance of URiM groups in orthopaedic surgery residency programs, with special emphasis on addressing barriers impacting the acceptance of Black orthopaedic applicants, because our findings suggest that the largest opportunities for improvement lie in Black orthopaedic resident growth rates and overall proportions relative to the total orthopaedic resident population. However, although the growth of Hispanic applicants and residents in orthopaedic surgery is promising, the proportional representation for the Hispanic population remains below that of other surgical specialties, suggesting the need for enhanced recruitment and acceptance efforts to achieve more-balanced representation of Hispanic people. Future studies might explore possible causes associated with the different growth rates between Black and Hispanic orthopaedic residents. Because this would likely revolve around the feedback of program directors and leadership, it might be best to consider designing or distributing a validated survey to obtain credible and valuable responses. Supplementary Material SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL abjs-482-1361-s001.docx (64KB, docx) abjs-482-1361-s002.docx (24.6KB, docx) Footnotes Each author certifies that there are no funding or commercial associations (consultancies, stock ownership, equity interest, patent/licensing arrangements, etc.) that might pose a conflict of interest in connection with the submitted article related to the author or any immediate family members. All ICMJE Conflict of Interest Forms for authors and Clinical Orthopaedics and Related Research® editors and board members are on file with the publication and can be viewed on request. Ethical approval for this study was waived by the Henry Ford Health Systems Institutional Review Board (IRB no. 16421). This work was performed at Henry Ford Health, Detroit, MI, USA. Contributor Information Alisha J. Williams, Email: alisha.williams@med.wayne.edu. Julia I. Malewicz, Email: julia.malewicz@med.wayne.edu. John M. Pum, Email: jpum1@hfhs.org. David Zurakowski, Email: David.zurakowski@childrens.harvard.edu. References 1. Abelson JS, Symer MM, Yeo HL, et al. Surgical time out: our counts are still short on racial diversity in academic surgery. Am J Surg. 2018;215:542-548. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education. ACGME data resource book. Available at: https://www.acgme.org/about/publications-and-resources/graduate-medical-education-data-resource-book/ . Accessed November 1, 2023. 3. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education. ACGME releases 2021-2022 statistics on graduate medical education programs and resident physicians. Available at: https://www.acgme.org/newsroom/2022/10/acgme-releases-2021-2022-statistics-on-graduate-medical-education-programs-and-resident-physicians/ . Accessed October 30, 2023. 4. Adelani MA, Harrington MA, Montgomery CO. The distribution of underrepresented minorities in U.S. orthopaedic surgery residency programs. J Bone Joint Surg Am. 2019;101:e96. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Anderson N, Nguyen M, Marcotte K, Ramos M, Gruppen LD, Boatright D. The long shadow: a historical perspective on racism in medical education. Acad Med . 2023;98(8 suppl):S28-S36. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Association of American Medical Colleges. ERAS statistics. Available at: https://www.aamc.org/data-reports/data/eras-statistics-data . Accessed November 1, 2023. 7. Association of American Medical Colleges. FACTS database and glossary. Available at: https://www.aamc.org/data-reports/students-residents/interactive-data/facts-glossary . Accessed November 1, 2023. 8. Association of American Medical Colleges. Holistic review. Available a