etween complicated and uncomplicated acute appendicitis feasible without imaging? Surgery. 2016 doi: 10.1016/j.surg.2016.04.021. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Salminen P, Paajanen H, Rautio T, Nordstrom P, Aarnio M, Rantanen T, Touminen R, Hurme S, Gronroos JM. Antibiotic Therapy vs Appendectomy for Treatment of Uncomplicated Acute Appendicitis. Jama. 2015;313:2340. doi: 10.1001/jama.2015.6154. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',17,'2025-10-13 08:48:42','2025-10-13 08:48:42');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1982,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8278363','The association between self-declared acute care surgery services and operating room access: Results from a national survey.','Ricci KB, Rushing AP, Ingraham AM, Daniel VT, Paredes AZ, Diaz A, Heh VK, Baselice HE, Oslock WM, Strassels SA, Santry HP.','2019','techs viscus looming catastrophe protocolized viscus tiering tiering skipping dichotomizing techs tiering techs investor tiering techs investor investor deferring techs unconsciously surveyors laudable nihms1673504 appendix_1 r01hs022694 hps heena santry santry lerro santry madore ayturk velmahos kiefe addiss tauxe pringle santry rebranding santry pringle kiefe khubchandani ingraham ayturk kiefe santry dabrowski kauder battistella ciesla cothren feurer guillamondegui pinson gosnell riskin brundage purtill sherck ekeh monson wozniak rubinfeld obeid azuh octogenarian grim weireter yaghoubian kaji ishaque rosing stabile virgilio santry janjua petrovick velmahos bukowiec brunsvold hemmila raghavendran sihler nontrauma matsushima tollack e143 weireter difronzo ciesla llerena pryor kauder dabrowski gracias braslow kamdar seese krapohl englesbe hemmila ttj esat ingraham ayturk kiefe santry ayturk kiefe santry boots ingraham khubchandani ayturk kiefe santry linnebur inaba chouliaras gmi mansfield demetriades nontrauma ahadataviewer dombrovskiy arumugam feurer guillamondegui pinson zenteno carnes nonelective vinden rangrej shariff kadzielski rubash bute grichnik andregg taekman aiken dinges brenneman rizoli tien earley pryor hedrick kurichi minogue sonnad ozegovic khadaroo widder wanis harington nihms1673504 appendix_1','Abstract Introduction: Timely access to the operating room (OR) for emergency general surgery (EGS) diseases is key to optimizing outcomes. We conducted a national survey on EGS structures and processes to examine if implementation of acute care surgery (ACS) would improve OR accessibility compared to a traditional general surgeon on call (GSOC) approach. Study Design: We surveyed 2,811 acute care general hospitals in the US capable of EGS care. The questionnaire included queries regarding structures and processes related to OR access and on the model of EGS care (ACS vs GSOC). Associations between the EGS care model and structures and processes to ensure OR access were measured using univariate and multivariate models (adjusted for hospital characteristics). Results: Of 1,690 survey respondents (60.1%), 1497 reported ACS or GSOC. 272 (18.2%) utilized an ACS model. ACS hospitals were more likely to have >5 days of block time and a tiered system of booking urgent/emergent cases compared to GSOC hospitals (34.2% vs 7.4% and 85.3% vs 57.6%, respectively; all p-values <0.001). Surgeons at ACS hospitals were more likely to be free of competing clinical duties, be in-house overnight, and cover at a single hospital overnight when covering EGS (40.1% vs 4.7%, 64.7% vs 25.6%, and 84.9% vs 64.9%, respectively; all p-values <0.001). ACS hospitals were more likely to have overnight in-house scrub techs, OR nurses and recovery room nurses (69.9% vs 13.8%, 70.6% vs. 13.9%, and 45.6% vs 5.4%, respectively; all p-values <0.001). On multivariable analysis, ACS hospitals had higher odds of all structures and processes that would improve OR access. Conclusions: ACS implementation is associated with factors that may improve OR access. This finding has implications for potential expansion of EGS care models that ensure prompt OR access for the EGS diseases that warrant emergency surgery. Level of Evidence: III, Therapeutic Keywords: Acute Care Surgery, Emergency General Surgery, Operating Room Access Introduction: Acute Care Surgery (ACS) was first formally proposed in 2005 as a health care delivery model, in part, to address the on-going shortage of general surgeons within the United States (US) and the lack of access to emergency general surgery (EGS) care.( 1 , 2 ) Inadequate access to care for conditions ranging from perforated viscus to necrotizing fasciitis has been deemed a “looming catastrophe” with more than 37% of emergency rooms reporting inadequate EGS coverage nationally.( 3 ) Since the formal conceptualization of ACS as a specialty including trauma, emergency surgery, and surgical critical care,( 1 , 2 ) implementation of this care model has varied throughout US hospitals.( 4 – 7 ) The effort to create a team-based, protocolized system that provides timely care to EGS patients remains a common thread from hospital to hospital.( 7 – 27 ) Overall, adoption of ACS models of care is increasing, despite a lack of national consensus among surgeons as to what particular structures and processes need to be in place for an optimal ACS model.( 4 , 7 , 8 , 28 – 30 ) Potentially life-threatening EGS diseases such as perforated viscus and necrotizing soft tissue infections rely upon timely intervention.( 31 ) As such, structures and processes to expedite OR access (e.g., over-night presence of operating room staff, EGS surgery block time, process for tiering emergency cases) and surgeon availability may ultimately improve EGS outcomes. We conducted a national survey on EGS structures and processes to better understand the implementation of ACS models in the US. In this manuscript, we focus on whether a self-declared dedicated EGS model (ACS) would increase OR access compared to a traditional general surgeon on call (GSOC) model. We hypothesized that hospitals utilizing an ACS model of care would have structures and processes in place that improve OR access. Methods: Data Source The methodology of the survey reported in this study has been previously described. ( 8 ) Briefly, the American Hospital Association (AHA) Annual Survey of Hospitals database was queried to identify acute care general hospitals caring for adult patients (≥ 18 years old) with a 24-hour ED and ≥ 1 OR. Review of hospital websites and direct outreach to hospitals was used to narrow this list to hospitals with EGS call coverage for at least part of the time. From August to December 2015, a hybrid mail/electronic 68-item questionnaire was sent in 2 waves—first, to senior surgeons who would have knowledge of EGS coverage at 2,811 acute care hospitals that met inclusion criteria and, second, to the additional senior surgeons or chief medical officers at hospitals with a single surgeon if a response was not received after the first wave of the survey. The questionnaire included multiple queries on hospital-level structures and processes (see Appendix 1 ) that would be expected to improve an institution’s ability to get patients into the OR in a timely fashion (e.g., over-night presence of operating room staff, EGS surgery block time, process for tiering emergency cases), hereafter to be referred to as “OR access.” In addition, respondents were asked to define their hospital’s overall approach to EGS care: “My hospital’s overall approach to EGS is [best described as]: ( 1 ) A dedicated clinical team whose scope encompasses EGS (±elective general surgery, ±trauma, and ± burns), ( 2 ) a traditional approach with an ad hoc general surgeon on call schedule, or ( 3 ) other.”) Hospitals that reported a dedicated team for EGS were classified as “ACS” hospitals for the purposes of analysis. Hospitals reporting that they did not have a dedicated EGS team and relied upon a traditional general surgery call schedule were classified as “general surgery on call” (GSOC) hospitals. Hospitals reporting “other” or skipping the question were excluded. Data from the AHA Annual Survey of Hospitals were used for queries that were not duplicated in our survey (e.g., number of ORs). ( 32 ) Statistical Analysis OR access structures and processes between self-declared ACS and GSOC hospitals were compared using Chi 2 tests of association, Student t-tests and Wilcoxon rank-sum tests where appropriate. As our goal was to identify which structures and processes that optimize OR access for urgent and emergent surgeries, responses on the Likert scale were dichotomized to “always” vs “not always” for analysis. Unadjusted and adjusted binary logit and cumulative logit regression models were then constructed to measure the odds of any given OR access variable being reported as always present. Models were adjusted for hospital ownership, location, inpatient bed capacity, teaching status, medical school affiliation, and trauma center designation as reported in the 2015 American Hospital Association Annual Survey.( 32 ) Given the complexities of OR block time and EGS daytime coverage, we used cumulative logit models for analysis rather than dichotomizing responses. Block time was analyzed with ≥5 days of block time as the referent (compared to 1–4 days and <1 day) and daytime coverage was analyzed with 5 or more consecutive days as the referent (compared to ad-hoc daytime shifts, 24-hour on call periods, and other). To determine appropriateness of models, sample size and standard model fit statistics were used. Tolerance statistics were used to assess collinearity between all hospital-level characteristics included in the self-reported model of EGS coverage. Tolerance statistics (all >0.2) were acceptable across all variables thus all were kept in the models. Analyses were conducted using SAS version SAS 9.4 (SAS Institute, Cary NC). This research was approved by the Institutional Review Boards of both the University of Massachusetts Medical School and the Ohio State University College of Medicine. Results: 1,690 US hospitals were represented in our survey data (60.1% response); 1,596 answered the query on the presence of a dedicated EGS service. 99 (6.2%) responded “other” and were not included in analyses. Therefore, a total of 1,497 hospitals are represented in the subsequent comparative analyses. 272 (18.2%) reported an ACS model while 1,225 (81.8%) reported a GSOC model. A majority of all hospitals (99.6% ACS vs. 96.2% GSOC, p = 0.007) had 24-hour OR availability. Therefore, this query was not further analyzed in comparative analyses. Table 1 compares the remaining OR access variables between ACS and GSOC hospitals. ACS hospitals were more likely than GSOC hospitals to have ≥5 days of block time for EGS, a tiered system for booking emergency surgical procedures, and a process to defer elective cases. Similarly, ACS hospitals were more likely to have surgeons who were free of other clinical duties when covering EGS during the day, who provided in-house overnight EGS coverage at night, who were not working covering EGS post-call, or who were not providing EGS coverage at multiple hospitals simultaneously overnight. Finally, ACS hospitals were also more likely to have in-house OR nurses, scrub technicians, recovery room nurses and anesthesia staff (all P-values <0.001). As seen in the ACS column of the table, however, even among those with a self-declared ACS model of care, there were substantial variations in structures and processes in place for OR availability, surgeon coverage, and overnight peri-operative staffing. Table 1. Differences in structure and processes as they relate to OR Access between hospitals with a dedicated emergency general surgery team model (ACS) and Hospitals with a general surgeon on call model (GSOC) for emergency general surgery coverage ACS Hospital N=272 (18.2%) GSOC Hospital N=1225 (81.2%) P-value * Operating Room Availability N (%) N (%) Number of operating rooms, (n, range) 19 [10, 34] 7 [4, 12] <0.0001 Block Time for EGS (%) <.001 <1 Block time 147 (54.0) 1027 (87.5) 1–4 Days 23 (8.5) 42 (3.4) ≥5 days 93 (34.2) 91 (7.4) Tiered system for booking emergent surgical cases (Yes) 232 (85.3) 706 (57.6) <.001 Process to defer elective cases (Yes) 217 (79.8) 818 (66.8) <.001 Surgical Coverage N (%) N (%) Daytime surgeons covering EGS free of other clinical duties (Yes) 109 (40.1) 57 (4.7) <.001 Daytime surgeon on call for EGS working post-call <.001 Always/Often 130 (47.8) 1120 (91.4) Someti