tab 1 <0.05 2 <0.01 3 <0.001 * As reported in 2015 American Hospital Association Database ** As self-reported in survey by study authors where ACS = A dedicated clinical team whose scope encompasses EGS (±trauma, ±elective general surgery, and ±burns) The results of our multivariable regression models are seen in Table 3a and 3b . Self-declared ACS was a predictor of all structures and processes that would enhance OR access as shown in Table 3a . Teaching status, urban hospitals, trauma certification, ACS hospitals and greater bed capacity were associated with increased likelihood of having in-house peri-operative staff overnight. GSOC hospitals had decreased adjusted odds in OR access structures and processes across every category (all P-values <0.001). Table 3b shows the results of the multivariable model with regards to both block time and daytime coverage. GSOC hospitals had higher adjusted odds of having no block time compared to ≥5 days of block time (3.84 95% CI [2.45–5.80]), while other hospital characteristics did not show a consistent pattern. Only the lowest capacity hospitals (fewer than 200 beds) had adjusted odds (4.48 95% CI [2.27–6.85]) of <1 day of block time. Intermediate level of block time (1–4 days) showed no statistical significance or pattern based on hospital characteristics when compared to ≥5 days of block time. With regards to daytime coverage, GSOC hospitals had much lower odds of having consecutive coverage ≥5 days in a row when compared to 24-hour coverage (0.32 95% CI [0.21–0.49]). As hospital bed size decreased, the odds of having ≥5 days of consecutive coverage decreased, however, this trend was not statistically significant. Minor and non-teaching status also had lower odds of having consecutive coverage (0.31 95% CI [0.17–0.58] and 0.39 95% CI [0.17–0.89] respectively). Ad hoc coverage did not show any consistent pattern or statistical significance when compared to traditional 24-hour coverage. Figure 1 visually demonstrates the ad