Unadjusted Odds ⬛ = Adjusted Odds Discussion: Patients with EGS conditions present without regard to time of day or day of the week. In many instances, these patients have truly life-threatening surgical emergencies such as gastrointestinal perforation or necrotizing soft tissue infection which account for the 20–48% of EGS operations that are performed at night.( 10 , 14 , 16 , 20 , 33 , 34 ) There is evidence that delays in surgical care for these conditions lead to poor outcomes.( 31 ) As a result, the ability to perform emergency operations round-the-clock these EGS conditions must be assured. In the present study, we examined a number of structures and processes that presumably enhance a round-the-clock access to the OR. We found that the presence of these structures and processes vary based on whether or not a hospital has established a dedicated model of care for EGS patients which we refer to as ACS. Our results suggest that establishing an ACS model of care that includes EGS patients with or without elective surgery, trauma, and critical care patients associated with more timely OR access. Factors at both the hospital system level and the workforce level contribute to getting a patient to the OR in a timely fashion. Some of these factors are unique from those involved in an elective surgery practice. A process to defer elective or less urgent cases is essential when a patient requires emergency surgery, in particular when OR resources are at capacity during typical daytime hours. Furthermore, dedicated block time for patients who require surgical intervention on an urgent yet not emergent basis (i.e., appendicitis, acute cholecystitis) has been shown to decrease time to surgery as well as improve OR efficiency.( 35 , 36 ) We found that ACS hospitals uniformly had tiered systems for booking emergent cases, processes for deferring elective cases, and greater amounts of block time. Interestingly, implementation of structures and processes to enhance OR access was more pronounced and consistent among ACS hospitals regardless of hospital size, teaching status, trauma certification, or ownership type. Therefore, it seems hospitals that proactively implement a team-based model of EGS care with or without other clinical service lines (i.e. trauma, critical care, burns) will have greater efficiency in booking cases and reducing operative delays. Even if processes are in place to expedite OR availability, surgeons are needed to perform necessary operations in a timely fashion. Our results showed that surgeons at hospitals using a GSOC model were less likely to remain free of competing daytime clinical duties, provide in-house overnight EGS coverage, and cover EGS at only one hospital overnight. Each of these processes could potentially decrease the timeliness of surgeon response to truly emergent EGS cases. Additionally, hospitals using a GSOC model were more likely to have surgeons providing patient care post-call. While the data on providing surgical care after working overnight is widely debated, ( 37 – 40 ) these findings may have quality implications for EGS patients who require semi-urgent daytime operations, such as appendectomy surgery at GSOC vs ACS hospitals. It should also be noted that hospitals using a GSOC model are less likely to have surgeons covering ICU and trauma overnight while also covering EGS. Based on our earlier work, in the present survey, we allowed respondents to self-identify as ACS if their hospital had a dedicated EGS service with or without trauma or elective general surgery or burns. Interestingly, our finding that ICU and trauma coverage overnight was associated with self-declared ACS status suggests that those locations are implementing ACS in a way that is consistent with the initial descriptions of ACS models where a single provider team would seamlessly provide care for trauma, EGS, and critically-ill patients.( 1 , 2 , 10 ) While further research is necessary to determine the impact of surgeon call schedules on clinical outcomes, previous studies have shown that hospitals that have implemented ACS models and in-house surgeon models have decreased ED wait times, improved timeliness to OR, and shorter lengths of stay for EGS patients.( 17 , 41 , 42 ) Surgeons are dependent on key peri-operative staff members to provide emergency surgical care. Across nursing and anesthesia staffing, we found that hospitals with ACS models of care were more likely to have in-house OR nurses, scrub techs, and anesthesia staff. This trend was also related to hospital size, as only the smallest hospitals had lower odds of in-house nurses and anesthesia providers. While the decision to operate is based on the patient’s clinical condition, it is possible that surgical timing may be influenced by the implicit association between the presence of in-house peri-operative staff as compared to staff that must come in from home in order to care for the patient during off hours. This, in turn, may have implications for subsequent day time OR utilization. ACS model implementation has been noted in several single center studies to improve daytime OR efficiency. ( 17 , 43 , 44 ) As we have previously reported, ( 8 , 29 , 30 ) our survey found that a majority of hospitals in the US have not implemented a dedicated team-based model of care for EGS patients. Among those that have implemented a self-declared ACS model there are substantial variations in the structures and processes in place for the care of EGS patients. In the present study, among ACS hospitals we found that a tiered system to book urgent cases (>85%), a process to defer elective cases (>79%), and an overnight surgeon also covering trauma (>76%) were in place at the majority of hospitals with ACS models. However, all other structures and processes that might improve OR access for EGS patients were in place at fewer than 75% of hospitals with a self-declared ACS model of care. The association between an ACS model of care and OR access structure and processes was stronger than that of any other hospital-level characteristic including hospital size, teaching status, trauma certification, or hospital ownership. This suggests that the implementation of ACS model is not merely the result of more resourced hospitals having the ability to do so, but rather how ACS hospitals harness existing resources to develop a team-based model dedicated to EGS care too allow for greater efficiency. There are limitations to this study. Given that the survey results are self-reported, there is a risk of response bias wherein the respondent may have consciously or unconsciously chosen the more ‘socially desirable’ option or the option they think the surveyors intended. Second, even though 60% response rate is laudable for survey completion, nearly 40% of hospitals that care for EGS patients are not represented in this study, and our results may not be generalizable to all EGS care settings. Furthermore, we applied a generous definition of the term ACS in this study to include any one of many approaches to a dedicated model of care for EGS patients based on our substantial pilot work in this area indicating that there is “no one size fits all” for ACS.( 30 ) Therefore, our findings may lack generalizability to hospitals employing the originally proposed model of ACS encompassing trauma, EGS, and surgical critical care. In spite of these limitations, to our knowledge this is the first study to investigate how the ACS model, as variable as it may be, is associated with enhanced OR access at hospitals nationally. Conclusion: Those who first proposed ACS did so, in part, out of a growing necessity to improve access to EGS care in the face of a shrinking general surgery workforce and declining hospital resources. Our findings suggest that providing EGS services in a manner similar to those originally outlined in the development of ACS may ultimately lead to improved patient outcomes by increasing OR availability, improving surgeon coverage, and assuring adequate peri-operative staffing. Further investigation is required to determine the effect enhanced OR access has on patient-level outcomes for the broad scope of EGS diseases. Supplementary Material Appendix 1 NIHMS1673504-supplement-Appendix_1.pdf (85.8KB, pdf) Acknowledgments Funding Statement This research is supported by a grant from the Agency for Healthcare Research Quality (R01HS022694) to HPS. The content represents the thoughts and opinions of the authors and not the funding agencies. The authors do not have conflicts of interest to disclose. Dr. Heena Santry had full access to all the data in the study and takes responsibility for the integrity of the data and the accuracy of the data analysis. Financial Conflicts of Interest Dr. Santry is a paid consultant by the Johnson & Johnson Company for service on a fragility fracture advisory board. The content herein is unrelated to the topic of the advisory board and is in no way supported by the Johnson & Johnson Company. References 1. Acute care surgery: trauma, critical care, and emergency surgery. J Trauma. 2005;58(3):614–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Sheldon GF. Access to care and the surgeon shortage: American Surgical Association forum. Ann Surg. 2010;252(4):582–90. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Rao MB, Lerro C, Gross CP. The shortage of on-call surgical specialist coverage: a national survey of emergency department directors. Acad Emerg Med. 2010;17(12):1374–82. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Santry HP, Madore JC, Collins CE, Ayturk MD, Velmahos GC, Britt LD, Kiefe CI. Variations in the implementation of acute care surgery: results from a national survey of university-affiliated hospitals. J Trauma Acute Care Surg. 2015;78(1):60–7; discussion 7–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Addiss DG, Shaffer N, Fowler BS, Tauxe RV. The epidemiology of appendicitis and appendectomy in the United States. Am J Ep